 hands and their  feet have a Ton Ton Ton of sensory  nerves which are constantly sending  feedback to and from the spinal cord to  to to and from the brain and a lot of  athletes they report that you know when  they're training really hard in their  feet get beat up their performance just  absolutely goes to [ __ ] and I really  think a similar thing may be going on  with the deadlift as well your hands  getting beat up your grip feeling really  fatigued you know anything you're doing  where you have to hold on to something  especially with a knurled bar for the  next few days you just get an unpleasant  sensation of holding on to that bar  which again perhaps via interacting just  with some reflex pathways via the spinal  cord maybe again a similar thing  decrease in central motor output that  very well could be something that makes  deadlifts take a little bit longer to  recover from just my own personal  experience with this when I'm dead  lifting with a fairly high volume if I  do most of my work with straps I find it  much much easier to recover from my  hands arm is banged up the next day and  my you know my training the next day is  probably going to suffer a little bit  but two days later I'm good to go again  three days later definitely good to go  again  whereas if I had a similar amount of  training volume without straps exact  same demand to my spinal erectors glutes  hamstrings everything else but my hands  are also really fatigued and beat up  from that session that's typically going  to then negatively impact my training  for the next three or four days so I  think that that's probably something in  play as well and then the last thing and  this this would probably be the biggest  one for newer lifters is just most  people when they pick up the bar for the  first time they're just much  bed lifters than they are squatters  deadlifts are much more similar to  things you've been doing your whole life  picking up something off of the floor  whereas you 